printSinkT -- SEQ 496 (Tony's standing order, 2026-10-31): CERr and COUt into PRINt. PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK before the
build, born red.

One print mechanism, three sinks. PRINt, CERr and COUt build the same instruct from the same print items; only the op
differs, and the op is the sink: print flushes to stdout or to printTO's divert, cerr to stderr, cout to stdout and
NEVER to a divert (KANT-23: that missing test is the feature).

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this
file's `stop();`, then the row on the OLD road (the statements themselves) or on the NEW road (`it1 = FILe("<row>");`,
the new top level, which runs as it builds), then a sentinel. Read by value: stdout, the stderr lines that are not the
harness's own, the REFUSED count, the exit status. The old road is the control, and every new-road row wants the old
road's value but one: PS-4, where the new road's own print rule decides (below).

TODAY (measured): CERr and COUt refuse by name on the new road ("waits for PRINt"), so every row prints nothing from them.

PS-1  cerr alone: 5 on stderr, nothing on stdout.
PS-2  cout alone: 5 on stdout.
PS-3  cerr between two prints: the prints on stdout, the 5 on stderr.
PS-4  a format and an expression through cerr: the same items print takes, and the NEW ROAD'S print rule -- a format
      applies to what follows (Tony, SEQ 449) -- so 2 then 3 in a width of 5: `2     3`. The old road puts the format
      on qa (`    2 3`), for cerr, cout and print alike; that is its control value, and it is not the target.
PS-5  a shortcut through cout.
PS-6  THE SINK ROW: print diverted by printTO, cout between. cout ignores the divert, so its 2 reaches stdout first and
      the diverted 1 prints after printTO(0). A cout that followed the divert would put both in the buffer.

PS-1|cerr 5:;
PS-2|cout 5:;
PS-3|print 1:; cerr 5:; print 2:;
PS-4|cerr qa #5d qb:;
PS-5|cout qa _ qb:;
PS-6|printTO(psBuf); print 1:; cout 2:; printTO(0); print psBuf:;
